        "title": "Immigration: an introduction",
        "text": "Migration takes many forms. People migrate as manual workers, highly qualified specialists, entrepreneurs or refugees, or as family members of earlier migrants. Whether they first intend to move temporarily or permanently, many migrants end up as settlers. Migration has been part of human history from the earliest times, but it has grown in volume and importance, and it may be one of the most important factors in global change.\n\nNo single cause can explain why people decide to leave their country and settle in another. It is hardly ever a simple individual act in which a person decides to move in search of a better life, leaves the home country and quickly becomes assimilated in the new one. Much more often, migration and settlement form a process that stretches over a long period, affecting the rest of the migrant's life and later generations too. It is a collective action, caused by social change and affecting the whole society of both countries.\n\nSome theories of migration emphasise individual choice, and others focus on the influence of governments and business, but it is more realistic to understand migration as a complex interaction between all these elements. Large-scale institutional factors, such as international relations, political economy and government policies, play a part in any decision to emigrate, and so do the informal social networks, practices and beliefs of the migrants themselves.\n\nMigration generally grows out of existing links between the home country and the new country, based on colonisation, political influence, trade, investment or cultural ties. For example, migration from some North and West African countries to France is linked to earlier French colonisation, which left French as a major language in those countries.\n\nTypically, migration is started by an outside factor, such as a shortage of labour in the new country. Often the first people to move are young, usually men. Once a path has been established, relatives and friends follow, helped by those who are already there. Social networks based on family or a shared place of origin help with housing, work, dealing with bureaucratic procedures and support in personal difficulties. These networks make the process of migration safer and more manageable for migrants and their families.\n\nMigrant groups develop their own social and economic infrastructure: places of worship, associations, shops, cafés, lawyers, doctors and other services. This is linked to families being reunited: the longer the original migrants stay, the more likely they are to bring in their partners and children or to start new families. People begin to see their lives as based in the new country. This is especially true of migrants' children: once they go to school in the new country, learn the language and make friends, it becomes more and more difficult for the parents to return to their homelands.\n\nAlthough the links between the immigrant community and the area of origin may sometimes weaken, they are more likely to continue over generations. Family and cultural links remain, and new business links may develop.\n\nAlthough every migration has its own specific historical patterns, some generalisations can be made about the way migrations develop. For example, most begin with young, economically active people going abroad temporarily. They want to save enough in a higher-wage economy to improve conditions at home, to buy land, build a house, set up a business or pay for education. After a period in the new country, some of these migrants return home, but others stay on.\n\nThis may be because they find living and working conditions in the new country better than in their homeland. But it may also be because of relative failure: migrants may find it hard to save as much as they need, and stay on in the hope of succeeding in the end. As time goes by, many of them are joined by their families or find partners in the new country. With the birth of children, settlement becomes more permanent, whatever the original intentions.\n\nThis can be summed up in a four-stage model. First, young workers move abroad temporarily to work, and send money home. Second, they stay longer, and social networks develop, providing mutual help. Third, the original migrants' families join them, there is a growing orientation towards the new country, and ethnic communities emerge with their own associations, shops, professions and so on. Finally, settlement becomes permanent."
